target: internal/mutate/mutate.go
verify: go test ./internal/mutate/ -count=1 -run 'TestCheckReportsEverythingThatWouldAbortARun/an_ambiguous'
expect: an ambiguous anchor was not reported
why:    exactly one occurrence, which is `one`'s own test and the reason this check can replace a run for the stale-catalog question. Zero means the catalog has rotted after a rename; MORE than one means the edit is ambiguous and `bytes.Replace(.., 1)` would change whichever occurrence comes first, which is code the mutation does not name. Both score Stale in a full run, so both are findable only after fifteen minutes without this gate — and the second is the one a reader drops, because `n == 0` reads as the whole problem. Weakening the test to `n == 0` keeps every stale-anchor case green and silently accepts the ambiguous one.
--- old
		n := bytes.Count(original, []byte(m.Old))
		if n == 1 {
--- new
		n := bytes.Count(original, []byte(m.Old))
		if n != 0 {
